Review written to `docs/reviews/types-physics/types-physics-07-dispatch-target-conflict-detection.md`.

**Summary of findings:**

| # | Severity | Issue |
|---|----------|-------|
| 1 | **high** | `conflicts_with()` returns `false` for `ByName` vs `ByEndUse` cross-variant comparisons, enabling priority inversion (Safety-tier overwritten by Schedule-tier) |
| 2 | medium | Conflict detection operates at target granularity only — orthogonal signals (e.g., SOC target vs power setpoint on same battery) are blocked, preventing legitimate coexistence |
| 3 | medium | Same-tier signals for the same target both pass through with last-writer-wins, no deterministic tiebreaker for competing DR or TOU signals |
| 4 | medium | `seen_targets` Vec accumulates duplicate entries per target per tier instead of using a HashMap to track max tier |
| 5 | low | No name↔end-use mapping infrastructure accessible to the conflict detector |
| 6 | low | Rejected lower-priority signals are permanently discarded — no fallback when a higher-priority signal expires |

The conflict resolution strategy is **priority-ordered with cross-pass ledger** (highest-tier-wins, lower-tier rejected). Compared to OCHRE, HARES misses two key capabilities: (1) OCHRE's routing layer normalizes end-use keys to equipment names before dispatch, eliminating the cross-variant gap; and (2) OCHRE performs fine-grained signal-level override (P Setpoint overrides SOC but doesn't block it entirely) vs. HARES's all-or-nothing target-level rejection.
